    faq=[
        ("Does Agile limit creativity?", "Done well, it protects creativity. Limiting work in progress reduces interruptions, clear priorities stop constant re-planning, and early feedback means less effort is wasted on directions that will be rejected. Timebox exploratory work rather than squeezing it out."),
        ("What is the best Agile framework for creative teams?", "Kanban is usually the best start for in-house studios, because creative requests arrive continuously and vary in size. Scrum suits goal-driven projects such as a website or campaign launch. Many teams combine them as Scrumban."),
        ("Is Scrum or Kanban better for designers?", "Kanban suits designers handling a stream of varied requests; Scrum suits designers embedded in a product or campaign team with a shared goal for each sprint. UX designers working with software teams often follow the team's Scrum cadence."),
        ("How long should a creative sprint be?", "Two weeks is the most common length for creative teams. The 2020 Scrum Guide allows up to one month. Shorter sprints give faster feedback but more planning overhead; longer ones risk late surprises."),
        ("Should creative teams estimate their work?", "Rough sizing, such as small, medium and large, is usually enough to plan capacity. Precise hour estimates are rarely worth the effort for exploratory creative work. Use cycle time data to forecast dates instead."),
        ("What is a design sprint?", "A design sprint is a five-day process, developed by Jake Knapp at Google and GV, in which a small team maps a problem, sketches solutions, decides on one, builds a prototype and tests it with customers. It answers one big question quickly and is different from a Scrum Sprint."),
        ("How do you manage feedback in Agile creative teams?", "Name reviewers and one approver in the brief, share rough work early, ask for feedback against the brief's objective, set a feedback deadline and have one person consolidate conflicting comments before they reach the creative."),
        ("How many review rounds should creative work have?", "Two rounds after the concept stage is a common agreement for standard work, with one more for complex pieces. Agree the number in the brief, track rounds on each job and treat extra rounds as change."),
        ("How do Agile creative teams handle urgent requests?", "Use an expedite lane on the board with a limit of one job, agree in writing what qualifies as urgent, and keep 20 to 30% of sprint capacity free for unplanned work."),
        ("Can video production use Agile?", "Yes, usually as a hybrid. Pre-production and shoot days need a fixed plan, while scripting and editing benefit from short review cycles on storyboards, animatics and rough cuts."),
        ("How do agencies use Agile with fixed-scope clients?", "They use phased scopes, retainers based on team capacity, and contracts that state the number of review rounds and how changes are handled. Clients are invited to regular reviews instead of seeing only finished work."),
        ("What columns should a creative Kanban board have?", "A typical board has Requests, Ready, Concept, Production, Internal review, Stakeholder review, Final artwork and Delivered, each with a simple entry policy and WIP limits on the active stages."),
    ],
